{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetry"],"declarations":22,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.map_bondSum_ne_semf_neutronMatter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.map_bondSum_ne_semf_neutronMatter","kind":"theorem","line":396,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L396-L415","doc":"**Class D does not escape invariant (2).** A fibre-level pair-additive bond\ncount whose rendered energies and counts are non-negative cannot reproduce the\nSEMF at pure neutron matter — for every coefficient ring, every ring-map\nrendering, every graph and every interface.\n\nThe escape hypothesis of class D was that #7165's statements are \"at order\nzero\"; this says that being at order zero was never what they used. What they\nuse is the shape and the sign of the *rendered* quantities, and a ring map\ndelivers the shape unchanged. A tower therefore reaches invariant (2) only by\nrendering some energy negative — which is class B, with class B's price\n(`BondCutSymmetryNoSignDefinite.enn_neg_of_semf_neutronMatter`) and class B's\nunchanged `δ ≤ 2 ln q` cap. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.map_bondSum_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.map_bondSum_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":386,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L386-L394","doc":"**A fibre-level bond count with non-negative rendered ingredients renders\nnon-negative.** `BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_nonneg` applied to the image, which is\nlegitimate precisely because `bondSum_map` says the image *is* a bond sum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.flavour_factor_to_leave_hull","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.flavour_factor_to_leave_hull","kind":"theorem","line":363,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L363-L380","doc":"**The price of leaving the hull, as a factor on the flavour split.** If the\nfibre-level tower renders the flavour split as `c · df₀` for a positive leading\nvalue `df₀`, then leaving the convex hull requires `c > 2/df₀`. With the\nmeasured `df₀ ≈ 1.4e-3` that is a correction factor above `1.4e3`: not a\nhigher-order term but a replacement of the leading one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.offsetRatio_mem_of_small","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.offsetRatio_mem_of_small","kind":"theorem","line":356,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L356-L361","doc":"The convenient direction: a small positive flavour split keeps the interface\ninside the hull. The corpus's `δ_f ≈ 1.4e-3` satisfies this with three orders of\nmagnitude to spare. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.offsetRatio_mem_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.offsetRatio_mem_iff","kind":"theorem","line":342,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L342-L354","doc":"**The whole of class D's remaining freedom, reduced to one scalar bound.**\nThe offset ratio lies in `[0, 1]` — the hypothesis of the `δ ≤ 2 ln q` cap —\nexactly when the rendered flavour split lies in `[-2, 2]`. Everything else about\nthe tower has already been absorbed by `interface_affine`, which does not see\n`z`, `h`, or any order of either. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.interface_flavour","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.interface_flavour","kind":"theorem","line":327,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L327-L332","doc":"The corpus's interface, as an instance of `interface_affine`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.offsetRatio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.offsetRatio","kind":"def","line":321,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L321-L325","doc":"**The corpus's offset ratio.** `SymmetrySignAmGm.weight_of_convention`\nderives the p–n interface letter as `σ - h·(1/2 - δ_f/4)` with `δ_f =\nΔm_pn/M_N`, so the offset ratio of `interface_affine` is `u = 1/2 - δ_f/4` and\nthe `BondCutSymmetryLogBound` weight is `w = 1 - u = 1/2 + δ_f/4`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.energySlope_tower","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.energySlope_tower","kind":"theorem","line":305,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L305-L319","doc":"**`δ_tower = Σ_k c_k · δ^{(k)}`.** The tower's p–n slope is the\ncoefficient-weighted sum of the per-order slopes — so \"all orders in `q`\" is a\nstatement about the *value* of `δ`, never about the *form* of the law. Through\n`BondCutSymmetry.bondSum_wellMixed_symmetry` the same holds of the symmetry\ncoefficient, whose only ingredient is `δ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.energySlope_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.energySlope_mul","kind":"theorem","line":299,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L299-L303","doc":"The slope is homogeneous in the energy triple. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.energySlope_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.energySlope_add","kind":"theorem","line":292,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L292-L297","doc":"The slope is additive in the energy triple. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.energySlope_map","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.energySlope_map","kind":"theorem","line":285,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L285-L290","doc":"**The p–n slope commutes with a field homomorphism.** `δ` is a second\ndifference divided by two, and a ring map between fields carries both. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.letterSum_tower","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.letterSum_tower","kind":"theorem","line":270,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L270-L275","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.interface_affine_map","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.interface_affine_map","kind":"theorem","line":240,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L240-L247","doc":"The identity is carried by any ring map, so the *rendered* interface is the\nconvex combination of the *rendered* like-species bonds with the *rendered*\nratio. Nothing about the hull is created or destroyed by the rendering. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.interface_affine_weight","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.interface_affine_weight","kind":"theorem","line":232,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L232-L238","doc":"The same identity in the `w = 1 - u` letters of\n`BondCutSymmetryLogBound.interface`, which weights `b_pp` rather than `b_nn`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.interface_affine","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.interface_affine","kind":"theorem","line":212,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L212-L230","doc":"**The interface bond is a convex combination of the like-species bonds, by a\nring identity.** For every Markov parameter `z`, every deformation `h` and every\noffset ratio `u`,\n\n```\nb_pn = (1 - u)·b_pp + u·b_nn\n```\n\nexactly. The identity does not mention `z` or `h` except through the three\ntraces themselves, so it is insensitive to the Markov convention, to the value\nof `q`, and to *every order of any fibre-level expansion of either*. Convexity\nof the corpus's interface is therefore not a numerical fact about `q₀` that a\nhigher-order term could spoil; it is an algebraic consequence of the three\nletters being one generator with three offsets. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.letterTrace","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.letterTrace","kind":"def","line":205,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L205-L210","doc":"**The two-strand Markov trace of the letter `σ + d`.** The trace is affine\nin the offset — `tr(c·σ + d) = c·z + d` for the Markov parameter `z` — and the\ncanonical letters all have `c = 1`, so the trace is `z + d`. This is the form\n`atom_braid_word_3A` emits: `pp = σ` (`d = 0`), `nn = σ - h` (`d = -h`),\n`pn = σ - h·u` (`d = -h·u`), with `h = q - q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.bondSum_tower","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.bondSum_tower","kind":"theorem","line":182,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L182-L201","doc":"**The tower decomposition: every order is separately a bond count.** If each\nenergy is a finite combination `Σ_k c_k · e_t^{(k)}` — a truncated fibre-level\nexpansion in any parameter, with the *same* coefficients `c_k` for the three\nspecies because they are orders of one expansion — then the bond sum is the\ncoefficient-weighted sum of the per-order bond sums.\n\nThis is the (D-co) reading of class D, and it is the reason the class buys no\nnew functional form: \"all orders in `q`\" produces a *sum of bond counts*, and a\nsum of bond counts is a bond count. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.bondSum_zero_energies","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.bondSum_zero_energies","kind":"theorem","line":174,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L174-L180","doc":"A zero energy triple gives a zero bond sum, whatever the counts. The base\ncase of the tower, recorded so `bondSum_tower` is visibly a decomposition\nrather than an approximation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.bondSum_mul_energies","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.bondSum_mul_energies","kind":"theorem","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L165-L172","doc":"**The bond sum is homogeneous in the energy triple.** Scaling all three\nenergies by a common factor scales the bond sum. Together with additivity this\nis linearity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.bondSum_add_energies","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.bondSum_add_energies","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L156-L163","doc":"**The bond sum is additive in the energy triple.** The counts are fixed, so\nadding two energy triples adds the bond sums. This is the property coefficient\nextraction preserves and a ring map's multiplicativity is irrelevant to. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.two_mul_bondSum_map","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.two_mul_bondSum_map","kind":"theorem","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L143-L151","doc":"The cut identity survives the rendering, since both sides are ring\nexpressions: if the degree-sum hypotheses hold in the fibre ring they hold in\nthe image, so the rendered law has the same one-parameter layout dependence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.bondSum_map","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.bondSum_map","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BondCutSymmetryFibreTower.lean#L131-L141","doc":"**`bondSum` commutes with every ring homomorphism.** The bond sum is a\npolynomial in its six arguments with integer coefficients, so any ring map\ncarries it to the bond sum of the images. Read right to left this is the whole\nof the (D-ev) reading of class D: forming the law in a fibre ring and rendering\nat the end gives exactly the same thing as rendering the ingredients and forming\nthe law at order zero. ","signatureChunk":0}]}